    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In recent years, there has been an increasing recognition of the extractivist data collection practices of technology companies, raising concerns about the protection of users' autonomy and privacy. In response, there has been a surge in research concerned with identifying the manipulative and coercive practices enabling companies to collect more data for their profit. Amidst these works, deceptive patterns are an object of intrigue. Deceptive patterns, also known as “dark patterns,” are user interface (UI) components that are designed to influence users' behaviors in ways that differ from their initial intentions (Brignull et al., 2010). While there is a rich diversity of research investigating the occurrence of deceptive patterns across different services and platforms, there remains a gap in research examining how these patterns manifest across three-dimensional immersive (e.g. virtual reality headsets) and two-dimensional non-immersive modalities (e.g. mobile phones). This honors thesis conducts a comparative study of deceptive pattern behaviors during the onboarding process in Meta and Meta Horizon Worlds to investigate whether deceptive patterns occur differently across modalities. After recording eight rounds of onboarding, my findings are consistent with previous research that has identified deceptive patterns in registration processes in social media. This thesis introduces fourteen novel deceptive patterns: Exclusionary Defaults, Liminal Borders, Virtual Guide, Verbal Frenzy, Audio Visual Frenzy, Mirroring, Suspicion Fishing, Flash Teaser, Glitching, Forced Rewind, Malfunction, Triggered Remodel, Haptic Invisibility, Haptic Affirmation. These findings raise concerns for user privacy and autonomy, addressing a need for empirical research that explores deceptive design implementations in immersive platforms.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
